PR #2571  overall: potential_risk
Spec: Smcdeleg/Ssccfg normative rules enforce that indirect counter access via siselect/sireg* is gated on menvcfg.CDE and per-counter mcounteren bits — without this gating, S-mode can leak M-mode-visible performance counter state
  [RISK] arch/riscv/include/asm/csr.h:214-231  (macro definitions)
      ENVCFG_CDE (bit 60 in menvcfg/henvcfg/senvcfg) is not defined. Every other xENVCFG feature bit (STCE bit63, PBMTE bit62, ADUE bit61, CBZE, CBCFE, CBIE, FIOM, PMM) has a macro, but CDE—the single bit that gates all Smcdeleg counter-delegation access—is absent. Without this macro, no kernel or KVM code can test or set the CDE enable gate. If M-mode firmware sets menvcfg.CDE=1, the kernel is blind to it.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/include/asm/csr.h:317-349  (macro definitions)
      CSR_SCOUNTOVF (0xda0), CSR_SCOUNTEREN (0x106), CSR_SISELECT (0x150), CSR_SIREG (0x151) are defined—the exact S-mode CSRs through which Smcdeleg counter delegation operates. No access-control wrapper, no CDE-aware guard, and no mcounteren-validated write helper exists around these definitions. Any code using these CSR numbers directly (csr_write/csr_read) operates without delegation-awareness.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/include/asm/csr.h:376-421  (macro definitions)
      CSR_HCOUNTEREN (0x606), CSR_HENVCFG (0x60a), CSR_VSISELECT (0x250), CSR_VSIREG (0x251), CSR_MENVCFG (0x30a), CSR_MENVCFGH (0x31a) are defined—the full hypervisor and machine-level CSRs in the Smcdeleg access-control chain. MENVCFG hosts the CDE bit, HCOUNTEREN is the hypervisor counter-enable gate. No CDE macro, no mcounteren mask macros, and no delegation-state tracking definitions accompany these CSR numbers.
